# STUDIO_SUITE_ARCHITECTURE.md
## Exhaustive Multi-Instrument Host Shell & Strict-Isolation Architecture Specification (Antigravity 2 Implementation Guide)

---

## 1. Executive Summary & Core Philosophy

This specification defines the **Web Audio Studio Suite Host Shell**—a lightweight, extensible multi-instrument web application launcher designed to host completely independent, standalone browser-based synthesizers, grooveboxes, and soundscape workstations under a single unified entry point.

### 1.1 The "Hard Product Isolation" Mandate
From a user, audio-engine, state-management, and persistence perspective, **each registered instrument MUST operate as a 100% independent product**.
- **Zero Audio Crossover:** Instruments NEVER share an `AudioContext`, master bus, clock scheduler, FX chain, or audio routing graph. Only **one** instrument may hold an active, running `AudioContext` and scheduler loop at any given moment.
- **Zero State & Storage Leakage:** Instruments NEVER share global Redux/Zustand/React state, `localStorage` keys, or `IndexedDB` object stores. Presets, patterns, BPMs, internal sub-modes, and recordings saved in Instrument A must never appear in, overwrite, or affect Instrument B.
- **Zero Cross-Imports:** Code inside `/src/instruments/<instrument-id>/` MUST NEVER import files from another `/src/instruments/<other-id>/` directory. All instruments communicate exclusively with the Host Shell via a strict **Instrument Plugin Lifecycle Contract**.
- **Preservation of Internal Instrument Sub-Modes:** Each registered instrument owns its entire internal viewport (`calc(100vh - 36px)`) and retains all of its own internal sub-modes, view toggles, and drawers without interference from the outer Host Shell. Specifically, **Product 1 (`groovebox`) MUST preserve its own internal `PLAY` and `STUDIO` mode switcher** inside `GrooveboxRoot.tsx`, switching views seamlessly without tearing down its own `AudioContext` or interrupting live playback.
- **Plugin Extensibility:** Adding a 3rd, 4th, or $N\text{th}$ instrument in the future (e.g., an FM synthesizer, granular sample mangler, or polyphonic ambient pad workstation) must require **only** creating a new self-contained folder under `/src/instruments/` and adding a single entry to the central `INSTRUMENT_REGISTRY`.

